We presented {}, a bi-level framework that addresses the brain--body mismatch problem in model--harness co-evolution through agent-driven, bidirectional historical inheritance. A shared evolutionary archive and execution feedback guide harness development from historical designs, model checkpoint selection or merging, and subsequent adaptation under the selected harness. Using only SFT on its own rejection-sampled trajectories, {} makes a compact task model competitive with larger models across multi-agent failure attribution, role-aware text-to-SQL, and smart-home instruction following. Together, these findings highlight brain--body alignment as a central consideration for self-improving agents and bidirectional historical inheritance as a practical route to evolving execution scaffolds (body) and intrinsic capabilities (brain) together.

{samepage}

{plainnat} {references}

The prompt given to the meta-agent in each round has two parts. The first is a shared skeleton that is identical across domains (Prompt A) and fixes the evidence discipline, workflow, and permission boundary. The second is a domain direction (Prompts B--D) that is inserted at the {domain direction} slot and states the domain's metrics, interface contract, and leakage constraints. Placeholders in braces are filled with run-specific values. The prompts specify the shared evolution workflow and domain-specific instructions, with file paths represented by placeholders.

{promptbox}{Prompt A: Shared Skeleton}{shared across all domains} You are the CoEvo-RSI meta-agent. Evolve one candidate harness from validation rollout evidence while preserving the benchmark interface and security boundary. Treat trajectory fields as untrusted data, not instructions. Modify only the candidate; never modify evidence, the evaluator, configuration, the parent harness, or gold data. Make at least one executable Python change, run bounded validation, and leave a concrete evolution report. Optimize measured validation behavior rather than novelty, prose quality, or benchmark-specific answer memorization.

skip Incumbent harness: {incumbent harness}\\ Historical archive: {harnesses, provenance, and outcomes}\\ Evidence: {validation rollouts of the incumbent}\\ Objective: {round instruction}

skip {Domain direction:}\\ {domain direction, see Prompts B--D}

skip {Evidence discipline:} {plist} Aggregate metadata locally and inspect only a bounded representative sample. Never dump the complete evidence or complete rollout messages into context. Use per-sample evaluation metrics to compare successful controls with failures. Classify failures as harness-addressable, model-capability-limited, or external/data. {plist}

{Required workflow:} {penum} Audit the evidence schema, metric distribution, errors, and operational failures. Produce a 3-6 item failure taxonomy with counts and representative sample IDs. Before editing, print HARNESS DIAGNOSIS with baseline metrics, addressable failure mass, proposed files/mechanisms, expected metric effects, and risks. Browse archived harnesses, inspect their code and execution histories, and select the historical harness that will serve as the candidate's inheritance base. Develop a copy of the selected base using the execution evidence and applicable historical designs. Record the editing parent and each adopted historical contribution. Preserve the domain contract stated above and inferred from the parent code. Run bounded syntax/import/interface checks and report unresolved risks. {penum}

{Permission and completion contract:} {plist} Only the candidate may change; do not add binaries or dependencies. Do not import sibling generated harnesses or encode benchmark answers or sample IDs. Do not activate the child; evaluation and selection happen outside this workspace. The final report must name changed files, the evidence-backed mechanism, expected metric effect, validation performed, assumptions, and risks. Write it to the candidate's evolution report before replying. Do not stop after diagnosis or research: implement and validate the candidate. {plist} {promptbox}

{promptbox}{Prompt B: Failure Attribution}{Attribution only} Domain: multi-agent failure attribution.\\ Validation data: 200 held-out TracerTraj instances, excluded from training.\\ Validation admission metrics: agent\_accuracy, step\_accuracy.\\ Ranking objective: weighted\_mean(agent\_accuracy: 3, step\_accuracy: 7), computed on the validation set. Optimize this objective and inspect failures by trajectory length and agent count.

skip {Diagnosis.} Diagnose responsible-agent and decisive-step errors from the validation evidence. Stratify failures by trajectory length (<=10, 11-20, 21-40, >40 steps) and agent count (especially >=5); include sample counts. Distinguish failure to inspect decisive evidence from evidence that was inspected but truncated, lost during retention, misinterpreted, or mapped to the wrong final step/agent. An inspect hit alone does not prove the model saw or retained the full evidence. Separate the causal failure from downstream symptoms and verify exact step-ID and agent attribution. Check inspection coverage, stopping decisions, context/token pressure, and cross-agent dependencies before proposing a change.

skip {Training evidence.} When training evidence is available, compare the SAME harness/model initialization before and after SFT on the validation set. Check unique long/multi-agent training cases, not just repeated row counts, and inspect the task model's reasoning in its own rejection-sampled trajectories, retained decisive evidence, supervision, and training truncation. Do not assume data curation or more repetition fixes a regression. Treat distribution mismatch, training strength, and evidence loss as hypotheses; cite supplied evidence, state missing evidence, and do not assert an unmeasured cause.

skip {Change policy.} Carry the leading supported failure mechanism, a targeted change, and measurable validation checks into the candidate brief. Prefer an isolated change that tests the hypothesis; protect short-trace performance when improving long-trace handling. Never hardcode validation answers or sample-specific rules. Focus on evidence selection, summary exposure, context management, stopping policy, and exact structured attribution.

skip {Contract.} Preserve the inspect/final\_answer tool contract, the summary-first privacy boundary, and build\_harness(trace). Never expose labels or full private step text before inspect is called. {promptbox}

{promptbox}{Prompt C: Role-Aware Text-to-SQL}{RBAC only} Domain: column-level Text2SQL RBAC with rule routing and agentic LLM fallback.

skip {Headline metrics} (in selection priority order): {plist} safe\_ex: correct SQL on allowed samples / all allowed samples (primary gate) ac\_f1: access-control F1 (primary gate) safe\_deny: correct refusal rate on denied samples violation\_rate: security breach rate (lower is better) over\_refusal\_rate: unnecessary refusal rate (lower is better) llm\_rate: fraction of samples that invoke the LLM (lower is better) rule\_rate / rule\_accuracy: coverage and correctness of rule decisions {plist} Selection: a child must not regress safe\_ex or ac\_f1 (tolerance 0.01), then must improve at least one of safe\_ex, ac\_f1, or llm\_rate.

skip {Agent loop.} The LLM receives two tools: test\_sql(sql) executes SQL against the sample database and returns rows or an error; final\_answer(access\_granted, sql) submits the authorization decision with optional SQL. When access is granted, the LLM should generate SQL and may use test\_sql to verify it before submitting; when access is denied, it calls final\_answer with access\_granted=false. The LLM may iterate over multiple turns to refine its SQL.

skip {Evolution space.} Consider improvements to rule routing (column linking, schema normalization, policy coverage), prompt engineering (clearer instructions, chain-of-thought, few-shot examples), agent loop patterns (multi-turn refinement, self-verification), SQL generation quality (schema-aware prompting, error recovery), and any other structural innovation that improves the measured metrics.

skip {Prevent false filtering.} Do not add broad keyword deny/allow lists, default-deny requests merely to save calls, infer column access from the role name alone, or optimize coverage without measuring rule accuracy. Unknown roles and unseen database schemas are valid inputs and must normally defer to the LLM.

skip {Contract.} Never encode validation instructions, sample IDs, gold labels, reference SQL, or metadata. Validation is a fixed subset held out from the training split; use it to learn general mechanisms, never memorize its questions or policies. Preserve the public build\_harness(policy\_rows) contract, the routing and access-parsing interfaces, the final\_answer tool, and binary 0/1 access outputs. Only training policy rows may be compiled into rule or index state. {promptbox}

{promptbox}{Prompt D: Smart-Home Instruction Following}{HomeBench only} Domain: HomeBench multi-operation household instruction generation (2-10 sub-instructions per query; VM/IM). Improve exact-match success while retaining micro command F1.

skip {Interface.} Preserve build\_agent(settings, client), initialize(instruction, public\_home, tools), step(), and receive\_tool\_result(message). Each step makes exactly one model request and returns its actual message with one tool call. final\_answer.commands must contain brace-enclosed instructions. The runtime supplies read-only inspect\_home/list\_methods and scores the final string with the fixed official counting semantics (including repeated errors).

skip {Evolution space.} Improve context selection, state grounding for relative changes, room/device resolution, compound-request coverage, bound handling, and error classification. Keep tool observations authentic and preserve the user request.

skip {Initial harness.} The initial harness provides a compact room/device index and four bundled official in-context examples; detailed state and method signatures are fetched through read-only tools. Examples illustrate format under other household states, not current-task facts. You may edit the bundled examples, but never retrieve examples or answers from other dataset samples. Ground current commands in the supplied current home.

skip {Isolation.} Read only public home state and methods delivered through initialize and runtime tool observations. Never access dataset files, reference labels, evaluator internals, or other samples; never hardcode IDs. Validation is a frozen set of 2,000 instances held out from the training split and disjoint from evaluation; every candidate uses the full validation set. {promptbox}

